## Autonomy Principles

AI should act autonomously by default. Before the design is approved, ask the user only when the answer cannot be determined after investigation, an unexpected situation prevents reliable progress, or the Design Gate approval is still pending.

After the design is approved, do not ask again: decide within the approved design, record deviations from it, and report only those in one final message (completion, deviations from the design, decisions needed). Stop mid-way only for the closed exceptions X1-X8 (review limit, release, irreversible operation, a design that cannot reach its goal, a failing E2E, unknown cause, broken state, something the AI cannot obtain) defined in `documents/development/development-policy.md` §1.0 "承認後の進め方"; in upstream skills (`superpowers:*`), "ask your human partner" after approval means the same (decide within the approved design unless one of these exceptions applies). X2 (release) includes a merge that by itself releases, publishes or deploys to production, and only an answer to that item or an instruction naming the operation and its target counts as its OK - not the design approval or a generic "continue". X3 (irreversible) includes deleting data or someone else's work, force-pushing, changing a shared environment, publishing externally, and pushing a fix for an undisclosed vulnerability to a public repository (use a private fork). Do not ask the user to run commands or do operations; run them yourself and verify.

Do not ask questions that can be answered by reading code, docs, command output, or existing project configuration. When errors occur, fix them durably; never use temporary workarounds such as disabling tests, swallowing errors, weakening types, or bypassing quality gates. If a durable fix is unclear, stop and ask (exception X6).

